WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.020
Today we are going to look at&nbsp;
NVIDIA’s new AI research work which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.440
as they say allows us to paint with words.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:21.480
So, let’s see. Yes, this runs a generative&nbsp;
denoising process, or in other words, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:28.740
starts out from a bunch of noise, and over time,&nbsp;
uses our text prompt to rearrange it into an image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:35.340
that we described. Great. Then, it subjects these&nbsp;
coarse images to a super resolution technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:41.580
which means that in goes this coarse image, and&nbsp;
out comes an image with so much more detail.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.420
And hence, can generate images of this quality.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:53.100
Now, wait a minute. We are experienced Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars over here, so we know that OpenAI’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.100 --> 00:01:00.000
DALL-E 2 can do this. And Google’s Imagen&nbsp;
can do this. The free and open source stable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.980
diffusion can also do this. We have a number of&nbsp;
papers that can pull this off really well. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:14.940
is NVIDIA a little late to the party? Why&nbsp;
publish this paper? What is new here? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.820
let’s push it to its limits through 3 really&nbsp;
fun experiments and find out together.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:28.380
One, it gives us something that many of us Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars desire. And that is, of course, more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:35.400
control over the synthesized images! For instance,&nbsp;
here, we wish to create an image of boxing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:42.420
squirrels. Yes, you heard it right. But wait, here&nbsp;
is the more granular control part: we can draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:51.120
exactly where each squirrel and the boxing gloves&nbsp;
go. And, there we go! Loving it. Or, if we wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:58.020
to create a rabbit who is also a magician, we can&nbsp;
also specify that it should stand on clouds, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:05.520
we noted that it should cast a fireball. Now we&nbsp;
can tell it where exactly that fireball should go.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:13.380
Two, it also follows our instructions really well&nbsp;
when it comes to requesting styles as well. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:20.760
ask it to paint this penguin in the style of many&nbsp;
famous artists, with really cool results. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.580
sometimes just saying which artist we are&nbsp;
looking for is not that helpful. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:33.120
which phase of the artist are we talking about?&nbsp;
Or which particular work should it be based on?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:39.120
And hold on to your papers, because this&nbsp;
can even help out when our words fail us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:46.440
How? Well, in this case, we can also use an&nbsp;
image instead. We can still add a text prompt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.340
and it will create the new image in the style&nbsp;
of this one. This is especially useful in cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.340 --> 00:03:00.780
when we have a style in mind that is really&nbsp;
hard to explain. I love this one. So cool!

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:07.380
Three, so how does it compare to the usual&nbsp;
suspects? Well, let’s have a look at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:14.700
teapots. Of course, to the surprise of no one,&nbsp;
Stable Diffusion and DALL-E 2 are both capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:21.720
of this task, however, look. We did not get a&nbsp;
painting of a panda. And, with the new technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:29.940
look at that! Oh yes! Once again, it follows our&nbsp;
instructions better. Now note that text to image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:35.940
AIs are not easy to evaluate as all models&nbsp;
can generate a ton of different images for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:42.240
the same prompt. However, further comparisons&nbsp;
reveal that there indeed is a pattern here.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.700
Now, have you noticed? There is a&nbsp;
pattern in this video too. I keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.420
saying that this new technique&nbsp;
follows our instructions better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:58.020
so here is the most important question.&nbsp;
Why? How is all this wizardry possible?

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:04.980
Well, this was one of my favorite parts of the&nbsp;
paper. Have a look at this. The authors claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:11.700
that as the classical text to image AIs start&nbsp;
out from noise, they follow our prompt closely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.880
however, later on in the image synthesis&nbsp;
process, not so much. If we change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.140
prompt for the last few percentage&nbsp;
of the noise diffusion process, look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:30.600
Ouch! It completely ignores it. And here comes the&nbsp;
best part: the authors trained multiple separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:37.380
denoiser networks that are suited to different&nbsp;
parts of the generation process. Hence, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.960
you guessed it right, these can follow our&nbsp;
instructions better throughout later parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.880
of the image generation process, and&nbsp;
thus, give us better artistic control.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:54.900
Now I am sure that the next generation of text&nbsp;
to image AIs are going to be even more powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:01.620
two more papers down the line, but this concept&nbsp;
may live on to improve even these subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:07.740
versions. I am very excited to see if this will&nbsp;
really be the case. What a time to be alive!

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.500
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

